Photochemical and thermal isomerization in the solid state The rotary behaviour of molecular motor 1 in the confined environment of the moto-MOF (Fig. 5a) was studied with Raman spectroscopy. This technique is particularly convenient, as it allows for the in situ monitoring of structural changes in the individual molecular components in the framework (that is, the molecular motor, TCPB). It is known for this particular family of MOFs that solvent removal leads to collapse of the porous structure41,47, so experiments were performed on moto-MOF crystals immersed in DMF. Photoswitchable MOFs, for example bearing azobenzene48,49 or diarylethylene50,51 struts, have recently been studied and proven to be stable following exposure to
## Conclusions
In summary, we have devised a simple strategy, using SALE, to achieve spatial 3D organization of light-driven molecular motors using a MOF platform. By using Raman spectroscopy, the photochemically and thermally driven rotary motion in solution could be compared to that in the crystalline solid. It is shown that the motor units in the struts are able to perform unhindered $360^{\circ}$ unidirectional rotations owing to a design that allows for sufficient free volume, provided by the chosen framework. These findings are remarkable, as it was shown previously that the incorporation of rotaxanes in MOFs influences their dynamics39,42. The successful preparation of a fully operational motorized MOF will allow, in the future, exploration of the collective behaviour of 3D-organized rotary molecular motors and tailoring of crystalline materials for a specific light-responsive function, for example, control of the diffusion of gases, directional light-powered mass transport or miniature light-powered pumps in microfluidic devices.
